WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.167
Welcome to Context, Tokens, and
Modalities. This beginner lesson covers

2
00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:08.333
tokens, context selection, multimodal
checks, a worked fictional office-safety

3
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:12.500
dossier, synthetic budget arithmetic,
independent practice, feedback, and

4
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.667
assessment. The dossier is teaching
evidence, not a real record

5
00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:20.833
or operational safety advice. Remember:
context is temporary, so important

6
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:25.000
facts may need controlled storage
elsewhere.

7
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.375
A token is a unit used to represent model

8
00:00:29.375 --> 00:00:33.750
input or output. It may be a whole short

9
00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:38.125
word, part of a longer word, punctuation,
a space, a

10
00:00:38.125 --> 00:00:42.500
code fragment, or another symbol. That
means a token count

11
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:46.875
is not the same as a word, character,
sentence, or

12
00:00:46.875 --> 00:00:51.250
page count. Two passages that look equally
long to a

13
00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:55.625
reader can be represented differently,
depending on the tokenizer and

14
00:00:55.625 --> 00:01:00.000
formatting. Tokens matter because products
enforce limits, measure usage, and

15
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.375
sometimes charge for input and output in
tokens. They do

16
00:01:04.375 --> 00:01:08.750
not measure a source's authority or
importance. A policy exception

17
00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:13.125
can be crucial even if it uses only a few

18
00:01:13.125 --> 00:01:17.500
tokens. When an exact limit or cost
matters, use the

19
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:21.875
provider's current counting method for the
exact model and interface.

20
00:01:21.875 --> 00:01:26.250
Do not rely on a universal words-to-tokens
conversion. In the

21
00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:30.625
later arithmetic, every quantity is
labeled ASSUMED because it is

22
00:01:30.625 --> 00:01:35.000
fictional planning data, not tokenizer
output.

23
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.529
The context window is the bounded working
information available to

24
00:01:39.529 --> 00:01:44.059
a model for a response. It can include
system instructions,

25
00:01:44.059 --> 00:01:48.588
conversation history, supplied documents,
tool results, and tokens generated during

26
00:01:48.588 --> 00:01:53.118
the response. Products differ in what they
include and how

27
00:01:53.118 --> 00:01:57.647
they manage long conversations. This
working set is not every

28
00:01:57.647 --> 00:02:02.176
fact learned during training, and it is
not a permanent

29
00:02:02.176 --> 00:02:06.706
database. A larger window can hold more
material, but size

30
00:02:06.706 --> 00:02:11.235
does not guarantee that every detail will
be used correctly.

31
00:02:11.235 --> 00:02:15.765
A buried exception can still be missed.
Conflicting text can

32
00:02:15.765 --> 00:02:20.294
still confuse priorities. Duplicate files
can add noise. More context

33
00:02:20.294 --> 00:02:24.824
is not automatically better context.
Improve the working set by

34
00:02:24.824 --> 00:02:29.353
stating the task, separating instructions
from evidence, labeling sources and

35
00:02:29.353 --> 00:02:33.882
trust levels, and retrieving only relevant
material when needed. For

36
00:02:33.882 --> 00:02:38.412
long work, summarize cautiously, recheck
source boundaries, and preserve durable

37
00:02:38.412 --> 00:02:42.941
facts outside the conversation. The
practical question is not simply

38
00:02:42.941 --> 00:02:47.471
whether material fits. Ask whether it is
trustworthy, relevant, clearly

39
00:02:47.471 --> 00:02:52.000
structured, and available when the next
decision is made.

40
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.400
Quick checkpoint. In one sentence, explain
why a larger context

41
00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.800
window does not guarantee correct use of
every detail. Name

42
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.200
one improvement, such as source labels,
clear priorities, bounded retrieval,

43
00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.600
or external durable storage. Compare your
sentence with this principle:

44
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.000
capacity does not replace selection and
verification.

45
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.353
Before sending a request, build a context
inventory. Record each

46
00:03:18.353 --> 00:03:22.706
candidate input, its role, trust level,
date or version, include

47
00:03:22.706 --> 00:03:27.059
or exclude decision, and reason. Start
with the objective and

48
00:03:27.059 --> 00:03:31.412
success criteria. Separate your
instructions from quoted reference

49
00:03:31.412 --> 00:03:35.765
material. Identify authoritative evidence,
supporting context, untrusted content, and

50
00:03:35.765 --> 00:03:40.118
unnecessary material. Remove duplicates
and unrelated files. Preserve important

51
00:03:40.118 --> 00:03:44.471
exceptions rather than shortening blindly.
When a corpus is too

52
00:03:44.471 --> 00:03:48.824
large, retrieve bounded relevant pieces
instead of pasting everything. This

53
00:03:48.824 --> 00:03:53.176
approach makes source boundaries visible
and allows later verification. It

54
00:03:53.176 --> 00:03:57.529
also reduces the chance that an old note,
rumor, or

55
00:03:57.529 --> 00:04:01.882
embedded instruction silently receives the
same weight as an approved

56
00:04:01.882 --> 00:04:06.235
policy. Keep stable facts, approvals,
versions, and operational status in

57
00:04:06.235 --> 00:04:10.588
a controlled external system of record. A
chat may help

58
00:04:10.588 --> 00:04:14.941
prepare a decision, but it should not
become the only

59
00:04:14.941 --> 00:04:19.294
copy of information that must persist. The
goal is the

60
00:04:19.294 --> 00:04:23.647
smallest complete packet that safely
supports the task, not the

61
00:04:23.647 --> 00:04:28.000
smallest packet at any cost.

62
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.313
Multimodal systems may accept text,
images, audio, video, or files.

63
00:04:32.313 --> 00:04:36.625
Each form needs its own quality, privacy,
and accessibility checks.

64
00:04:36.625 --> 00:04:40.938
An image may be cropped, blurred, low
contrast, or blocked

65
00:04:40.938 --> 00:04:45.250
by glare. Audio may be noisy, omit a
speaker label,

66
00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:49.563
or contain overlapping voices. Video adds
timing and scene boundaries.

67
00:04:49.563 --> 00:04:53.875
A file parser may omit a table, reorder
columns, lose

68
00:04:53.875 --> 00:04:58.188
footnotes, or extract text without its
layout. Metadata may reveal

69
00:04:58.188 --> 00:05:02.500
sensitive information. First, confirm what
the system actually received. Then

70
00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.813
provide an accessible text alternative,
such as alt text, a

71
00:05:06.813 --> 00:05:11.125
transcript, or a table description. Verify
consequential claims against the

72
00:05:11.125 --> 00:05:15.438
original artifact. A generated description
of an image or recording

73
00:05:15.438 --> 00:05:19.750
is an interpretation, not the artifact
itself. If a region

74
00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:24.063
is hidden, say UNKNOWN rather than
reconstructing it. This class

75
00:05:24.063 --> 00:05:28.375
uses spoken narration with static text and
descriptions. It does

76
00:05:28.375 --> 00:05:32.688
not provide rendered images, audio
evidence, interactive hiding, or automatic

77
00:05:32.688 --> 00:05:37.000
gating.

78
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:40.333
The transcript and captions must reproduce
these spoken words verbatim,

79
00:05:40.333 --> 00:05:43.667
and the text-only and reduced-motion
versions must preserve the same

80
00:05:43.667 --> 00:05:47.000
information.

81
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:51.429
Now apply those rules to the fictional
office-safety review. Source

82
00:05:51.429 --> 00:05:55.857
P-01, dated 2026-09-01, is the
authoritative approved policy excerpt. It

83
00:05:55.857 --> 00:06:00.286
